# 4.14 LAB — Build a Prompt-Injection Defense

## Scenario

You are building an AI customer-service agent.

The agent can:

- search customer records;
- retrieve order information;
- draft emails;
- create support tickets.

### Security objective

Prevent malicious user or document content from causing unauthorized actions.

---

## Step 1 — Identify Trust Levels

Classify:

| Input | Trust |
|---|---|
| System policy | Trusted |
| Application configuration | Trusted |
| Authenticated user identity | Controlled |
| User message | Untrusted |
| Retrieved document | Untrusted |
| External webpage | Untrusted |

---

## Step 2 — Define Tool Permissions

Create a permission table.

| Tool | Agent access | Approval |
|---|---|---|
| Search order | Yes | No |
| Read customer record | Limited | No |
| Draft email | Yes | No |
| Send email | Restricted | Yes |
| Delete record | No | Human only |

---

## Step 3 — Build Output Validation

Before executing an action, validate:

1. requested action;
2. user authorization;
3. target resource;
4. parameter values;
5. policy;
6. risk level.

---

## Step 4 — Create Test Cases

Use harmless test prompts such as:

- conflicting instructions;
- fake system messages;
- malicious text embedded in a document;
- attempts to override application policy;
- requests for unauthorized operations.

The goal is not to attack a production service.

The goal is to verify whether your controls correctly identify untrusted instructions.

---

